// Canonical vicinae settings, imported by ~/.config/vicinae/settings.json.
//
// Why this is a separate file rather than settings.json itself: the vicinae GUI
// writes to settings.json every time a preference is changed there, and a
// chezmoi-managed file that a running application rewrites is a file that is
// permanently "modified" and gets clobbered on the next apply. Imports are the
// escape hatch vicinae documents for exactly this — imported files are merged
// before the user config and are never touched by vicinae.
//
// The consequence, and it is the right way round: anything set through the GUI
// lands in settings.json and therefore *overrides* what is here. This file is
// the machine's starting position, not a cage.
//
// It is deliberately short. Everything else is left at the vicinae default,
// including the window geometry and decorations — an earlier version forced a
// 24px radius and disabled client-side decorations to imitate the rofi window,
// which is not what we are aiming for. `vicinae config default` prints the full
// annotated set of options to pick from when something does need changing.
{
  // Vicinae maps a theme to each system appearance and follows the XDG portal's
  // colour-scheme, so Mod+N moves it with the rest of the desktop without
  // anything having to tell it to — apply-dark-mode.sh flips the portal and
  // stops there.
  //
  // These are vicinae's own bundled themes, used as shipped. There is
  // deliberately no local override: a wrapper theme that only declared
  // `inherits` still did not render identically to the built-in it inherited
  // from (visible on hover in the Mod+R theme selector), so wrapping costs
  // fidelity and buys nothing until there is an actual colour to change.
  //
  // Mocha/Latte rather than Frappé or Macchiato because that is the pairing the
  // rest of the desktop already uses — see the cursor themes
  // (catppuccin-mocha-dark-cursors / catppuccin-latte-light-cursors).
  //
  // Ids are the filenames under /usr/share/vicinae/themes/ without the .toml,
  // not the `[meta] name` inside them ("Catppuccin Mocha").
  "theme": {
    "dark": {
      "name": "catppuccin-mocha",
      "icon_theme": "auto"
    },
    "light": {
      "name": "catppuccin-latte",
      "icon_theme": "auto"
    }
  }
}
